  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =6\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)+C \\\\ 4 & =6(0)+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=6\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+4.\n$$",
